---
description: Kernel and xPlugin boundaries for Xenakis
alwaysApply: true
---

# Architecture

- Kernel code must not name an xPlugin (`xen.groups`, `xen.paths`, `xen.transport`, `xen.osc`, or any `xplugins/` include). Register features only through `XPlugin` and the generated `createXPlugins()`.
- An xPlugin includes only `kernel/` and `contracts/` headers. It must not include or link another xPlugin. `python tools/xplugin lint` enforces this.
- An xPlugin writes only its own components, via commands. Properties go through the property registry (`SetProperty`), not ad-hoc setters.
- `rt/` functions that run on the control clock are `noexcept` and do not allocate, lock, or throw. Build snapshot data in `compile` on the message thread.
- `ui/` is a separate target behind `if(NOT XEN_HEADLESS)`. Do not link it into `xenakis` or `xen_tests`.
- Message-thread types (`Document`, `CommandStack`, `EventBus`) are not used from the clock thread. The clock reads the published snapshot only.
- Prefer adding a contract in `contracts/` over a cross-xPlugin call. Do not add `TempoSource` until `xen.transport`.

```cpp
// Bad: kernel or xen.osc includes groups to ask if a group is muted.
#include "GroupsXPlugin.h"

// Good: read the component JSON the owner documented.
auto* group = document.findComponent(id, "xen.groups", "Group");
```
